'use client';

import {useEffect,useMemo,useState} from 'react';
import {BookOpen,ClipboardCopy,HardDrive,Plus,Search,ShieldAlert,Trash2,Terminal,Pencil,Copy,RotateCcw} from 'lucide-react';
import {Button} from '@/components/ui/button';
import {Input} from '@/components/ui/input';
import {Textarea} from '@/components/ui/textarea';
import {Label} from '@/components/ui/label';
import {Badge} from '@/components/ui/badge';
import {Dialog,DialogContent,DialogDescription,DialogFooter,DialogHeader,DialogTitle} from '@/components/ui/dialog';
import {toast} from 'sonner';

type ToolkitKind='guide'|'playbook'|'command';
type Platform='Splunk'|'Microsoft Defender'|'Cortex XDR';
type Query={platform:Platform;title:string;query:string};
type ToolkitItem={id:string;kind:ToolkitKind;title:string;category:string;summary:string;content:string;command:string;queries:Query[]};
type EditorState=Omit<ToolkitItem,'id'>;
const STORAGE_KEY='csirt.dfir-toolkit.v1';
const platforms:Platform[]=['Splunk','Microsoft Defender','Cortex XDR'];

const builtins:ToolkitItem[]=[
 {id:'guide-windows-execution',kind:'guide', title:'Windows Execution Artifacts',category:'Windows · Session 0 · 2026-10-20',summary:'Triage reference for Prefetch, Amcache, Shimcache, BAM/DAM, UserAssist, PCA and SRUM.',content:`GOLDEN RULE
 Presence ≠ execution ≠ execution timestamp. Corroborate with at least two independent sources and state the confidence level.

PREFETCH — C:\\Windows\\Prefetch\\<EXE>-<HASH>.pf
 Generally indicates execution; contains a run counter, up to 8 last-run timestamps on recent Windows versions, and the files accessed during process start-up. The hash depends on the path; for some hosts (svchost, rundll32, mmc…), the command line can contribute to the hash. A renamed binary appears under its launch name. Recent versions may compress Prefetch files (MAM signature). Servers may have Prefetch disabled. Observed maximum: 1,024 entries (Windows 8+); 128 on Windows 7.
 Activation: HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Session Manager\\Memory Management\\PrefetchParameters\\EnablePrefetcher (0 disabled, 3 applications and boot).
 An empty folder is a finding to explain, not proof of anti-forensics: look for deletion in $UsnJrnl and verify the configuration.

AMCACHE — C:\\Windows\\AppCompat\\Programs\\Amcache.hve
 InventoryApplicationFile provides, among other things, LowerCaseLongPath, FileId/SHA1, Size, LinkDate, publisher and original name. LinkDate is a date taken from the PE header, not the arrival date or the execution date. The compatibility inventory can record a file that was never launched. The SHA1 may cover only the first 30 MB. Collect Amcache.hve.LOG1 and LOG2 along with the hive.

SHIMCACHE — SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Session Manager\\AppCompatCache
Does not on its own prove execution on Windows 10/11; the associated date is the file's last modification time, not its launch time. The cache is held in memory and written to SYSTEM at shutdown/reboot: a live triage may not reflect events since the last shutdown. Acquire RAM before any action; do not reboot to "flush".

BAM/DAM — SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\bam\\State\\UserSettings\\<SID>
On Windows 10 1809+, the state is under State\\UserSettings (earlier versions: UserSettings). Can provide the device path and the last-execution FILETIME, with SID attribution. Updates at process end have been observed: validate this behavior for the build concerned.

USERASSIST / PCA
UserAssist under NTUSER.DAT\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Explorer\\UserAssist\\{GUID}\\Count: indicator of GUI launches; ROT13 values and binary structure. Known GUIDs: {CEBFF5CD-ACE2-4F4F-9178-9926F41749EA} (executables) and {F4E57C4B-2036-45F0-A9AB-443BCFE33D9F} (shortcuts). In the observed 72-byte structure: run count at offset 4, focus time at 12, last-execution FILETIME at 60. Verify against the OS and the parser. Not expected for a CLI launch. PCA (Windows 11 22H2+) can contribute for launches via Explorer; review PcaAppLaunchDic.txt and PcaGeneralDb*.txt. The absence of these traces does not mean the absence of execution.

SRUM — C:\\Windows\\System32\\sru\\SRUDB.dat
The tables D10CA2FE-6FCF-4F6D-848E-B2E99266FA89 (resource usage) and 973F5D5C-1D90-4944-BE8E-24B94231A174 (bytes per application/SID) can link application, user and bytes transferred at roughly hourly granularity, without identifying the network destination. Corroborate with firewall, proxy, DNS and XDR. Buffering, flush (~1 h) and retention (~30–60 days) depend on the system and its configuration. Dirty database: any esentutl /r sru /i recovery must be done on a COPY, never on the original.

CORROBORATION & TIMELINE
File creation is determined from $MFT ($FN), not from Amcache LinkDate or the Shimcache timestamp. Check $UsnJrnl for creation/deletion, 4688 with command line, Sysmon EID 1, EDR/XDR telemetry and authentication logs. The parent process is demonstrated with the EDR causality chain, 4688 or Sysmon, not by assumption.

ANALYSIS & PRESERVATION
Run parsers on a working copy and document the tool/version, time zone, hash and chain of custody. Column names and tool options vary: validate the help output of the installed version. For Velociraptor, query a limited sample first and confirm the columns before any wide collection.
A large binary with no publisher metadata, touching sensitive directories with a neighboring configuration, deserves in-depth examination (e.g. a renamed sync tool); this is not attribution on its own. An rclone configuration may contain reversible secrets: treat it as sensitive evidence, restrict access and never paste it into a shared template.

CONFIDENCE LEVEL
Low: indirect artifact or uncertain configuration. Medium: consistent artifact corroborated by an independent source. High: several independent traces agree on the host, the user and the time window. State the limitations and missing sources explicitly.`,command:'',queries:[]},
 {id:'guide-evidence',kind:'guide',title:'Collection & Interpretation Tips',category:'Method · Evidence quality',summary:'Preserve context, distinguish observation from conclusion, and document limitations.',content:`BEFORE TOUCHING THE HOST
Confirm the scope, the authorization, the incident contact and the evidence strategy. Record local time and UTC, the clock offset, the asset identifier and the operator. Photograph or note the initial state. In case of volatile activity, decide with the incident lead the order of RAM, connections, processes, logs and disk; do not reboot out of reflex.

CHAIN OF CUSTODY
Assign a unique identifier to each item. Record source, method, tool/version, start and end time, operator, storage and transfers. Compute SHA-256 as soon as possible, keep the original read-only and analyze a verified copy. Document any deviation or acquisition error.

TIMESTAMPS
Keep the raw value, the UTC interpretation and the original time zone. Distinguish system time, file time (MACE), event time and source precision. Do not compare timestamps before checking NTP drift, DST, boot and the semantics of the artifact.

CONCLUSIONS
Separate observed facts from hypotheses and assessment. Presence does not establish execution; execution does not on its own prove intent or impact. Cite corroborating sources, contradicting artifacts, the confidence level and collection limitations.

REMOTE COLLECTION
Favor approved procedures, minimize changes to the asset and keep a record of each command and its result. Do not perform repair, cleanup or remediation before the agreed preservation. Restrict exports containing personal data and comply with applicable notification and retention rules.`,command:'',queries:[]},
 {id:'guide-logon9',kind:'guide',title:'Logon Type 9 & Alternate Credentials',category:'Windows · Identity',summary:'Interpret NewCredentials and search for related events without over-concluding.',content:`4624 type 9 (NewCredentials) corresponds in particular to runas /netonly on the source machine: the local context remains that of the local user, while alternate credentials are used for network access. Look for an associated 4648 and examine the Network Account / Network Domain fields.

This type can also appear in other delegated-credential scenarios or administration tools; it is not standalone proof of Mimikatz, Cobalt Strike or malicious activity. On a user workstation, investigate the initiating process, the command line, the account, the destination and network logs.

Correlate Security 4624/4648, 4688 or Sysmon EID 1, XDR telemetry and remote authentication activity. Document the host where the event was generated: type 9 appears on the source.`,command:'',queries:[]},
 {id:'guide-mssp',kind:'guide',title:'MSSP Context & Notifications',category:'MSSP · Belgium',summary:'Document telemetry coverage by asset type and quickly coordinate applicable obligations.',content:`TELEMETRY COVERAGE
On Windows servers, Prefetch may be disabled. List, per asset type, the sources actually available: Amcache, Shimcache, BAM, 4688 auditing with command line, endpoint logs and Cortex XDR. Note retention, collection windows, access rights and gaps in the client record.

MULTI-CLIENT TRIAGE
Verify the tenant, the client and the scope before any search or export. Separate data and evidence by client, restrict access, record the operator and have containment actions validated by the designated lead. A cross-client search must be authorized and logged.

NOTIFICATION
The NIS2 framework generally provides for an early warning to the CCB within 24 h and an incident notification within 72 h, with specific obligations and deadlines depending on the entity and the applicable Belgian framework. In case of a personal data breach, the GDPR in principle requires notification to the DPA within 72 h of becoming aware of it, if the notification conditions are met. Have applicability, the starting point and the procedures confirmed by the DPO/legal; this sheet is not legal advice.

THREAT INTELLIGENCE
Open sources such as The DFIR Report, its indicators and Sigma/YARA/Suricata rules can feed threat intelligence. Validate provenance, date, confidence and false positives before integration into the SIEM/XDR; adapt rules with a test set and a controlled scope.`,command:'',queries:[]},
 {id:'playbook-ransomware',kind:'playbook',title:'Ransomware & Encryption',category:'Malware · Impact',summary:'Preserve evidence, identify the initial vector, measure propagation and prepare a validated recovery.',content:`1. Coordinate isolation with the incident lead; do not power off if memory or volatile activity is needed.
2. Capture RAM and connections per the approved procedure; preserve endpoint, identity, firewall, backup and hypervisor logs.
3. Identify patient zero, the process tree, the initial access mechanism and the timeline.
4. Look for remote execution, privileged accounts, tasks/services, disabled backup tools and lateral movement.
5. Quantify affected hosts/files, staged or exfiltrated data and the state of backups.
6. Block confirmed indicators, revoke compromised sessions/credentials and monitor re-entry paths.
7. Restore only from verified backups, then document the impact, notifications and exit criteria.`,command:'',queries:[
  {platform:'Splunk',title:'Process / indicator on host',query:'index=edr earliest={{START}} latest={{END}} (host="{{HOSTNAME}}" OR dest="{{HOSTNAME}}") ("{{IOC}}" OR "{{IP}}") | table _time host user process_name process_path process_command_line parent_process_name'},
  {platform:'Microsoft Defender',title:'Suspicious processes',query:'DeviceProcessEvents\n| where Timestamp between (datetime({{START}}) .. datetime({{END}}))\n| where DeviceName =~ "{{HOSTNAME}}" or ProcessCommandLine contains "{{IOC}}"\n| project Timestamp, DeviceName, AccountName, FileName, FolderPath, ProcessCommandLine, InitiatingProcessFileName\n| order by Timestamp desc'},
  {platform:'Cortex XDR',title:'Processes and command line',query:'dataset = xdr_data\n| filter event_type = ENUM.PROCESS\n| filter _time >= to_timestamp("{{START}}") and _time <= to_timestamp("{{END}}")\n| filter agent_hostname ~= "(?i){{HOSTNAME}}" or action_process_image_command_line ~= "(?i){{IOC}}"\n| fields _time, agent_hostname, actor_effective_username, action_process_image_name, action_process_image_path, action_process_image_command_line'}
 ]},
 {id:'playbook-bec',kind:'playbook', title:'Business Email Compromise (BEC)',category:'Identity · Email',summary:'Secure the identity, preserve tenant traces and hunt for fraudulent rules and messages.',content:`1. Preserve audit, sign-in and mailbox logs and the relevant messages before they expire.
 2. Revoke sessions and tokens, reset credentials per procedure and verify MFA / authentication methods.
 3. Review forwarding rules, delegations, OAuth permissions, consents and security changes.
 4. Search for similar messages, recipients, hijacked conversations and payment instructions.
 5. Determine the data accessed, the exposure window and the affected correspondents; coordinate with finance and legal.
 6. Monitor for recurring sign-ins, consents and changes; notify according to applicable obligations.`,command:'',queries:[
  {platform:'Splunk',title:'Authentication activity by account',query:'index=identity earliest={{START}} latest={{END}} (user="{{EMAIL}}" OR src_user="{{EMAIL}}") | table _time user action result src_ip app authentication_method'},
  {platform:'Microsoft Defender',title:'Sign-ins and email activity',query:'let account = "{{EMAIL}}";\nCloudAppEvents\n| where Timestamp between (datetime({{START}}) .. datetime({{END}}))\n| where AccountId =~ account or RawEventData has account\n| project Timestamp, AccountId, ActionType, IPAddress, UserAgent, Application, RawEventData\n| order by Timestamp desc'},
  {platform:'Cortex XDR',title:'Identity events',query:'dataset = xdr_data\n| filter event_type = ENUM.LOGIN\n| filter _time >= to_timestamp("{{START}}") and _time <= to_timestamp("{{END}}")\n| filter actor_effective_username ~= "(?i){{EMAIL}}" or action_remote_ip ~= "{{IP}}"\n| fields _time, agent_hostname, actor_effective_username, action_remote_ip, action_type, action_status'}
 ]},
 {id:'playbook-exfiltration',kind:'playbook', title:'Data Exfiltration',category:'Impact · Network',summary:'Link identity, application, accessed data and network destinations.',content:`1. Define the data and time period; preserve proxy, DNS, firewall, DLP, cloud audit, endpoint and identity logs.
 2. Identify staging, archive, tool/transfer method and the effective account; compare against expected behavior.
 3. Link bytes and activity to an application/user (SRUM can help, but does not provide the destination).
 4. Confirm the destination with proxy/firewall/DNS/XDR; distinguish observed transfer from merely suspected transfer.
 5. Quantify the data that may have left the environment and qualify the uncertainties.
 6. Contain the active transfer and revoke the compromised identity; review contractual and regulatory obligations.`,command:'',queries:[
  {platform:'Splunk',title:'Network requests to an IOC',query:'index=proxy OR index=firewall earliest={{START}} latest={{END}} (src="{{IP}}" OR dest="{{IP}}" OR user="{{EMAIL}}") | table _time src dest src_ip dest_ip user bytes_in bytes_out action url'},
  {platform:'Microsoft Defender',title:'Outbound network connections',query:'DeviceNetworkEvents\n| where Timestamp between (datetime({{START}}) .. datetime({{END}}))\n| where DeviceName =~ "{{HOSTNAME}}" or RemoteIP == "{{IP}}" or InitiatingProcessAccountName =~ "{{EMAIL}}"\n| project Timestamp, DeviceName, InitiatingProcessAccountName, InitiatingProcessFileName, RemoteUrl, RemoteIP, RemotePort, Protocol\n| order by Timestamp desc'},
  {platform:'Cortex XDR',title:'Network traffic by host / destination',query:'dataset = xdr_data\n| filter event_type = ENUM.NETWORK\n| filter _time >= to_timestamp("{{START}}") and _time <= to_timestamp("{{END}}")\n| filter agent_hostname ~= "(?i){{HOSTNAME}}" or action_remote_ip ~= "{{IP}}"\n| fields _time, agent_hostname, actor_effective_username, action_local_ip, action_remote_ip, action_remote_port, action_network_bytes_sent, action_network_bytes_received'}
 ]},
 {id:'playbook-execution',kind:'playbook', title:'Suspicious Execution & Renamed Binary',category:'Windows · Malware',summary:'Establish whether, when, by whom and from which parent a binary was launched.',content:`1. Preserve the binary and its metadata; compute SHA-256 and record the original path.
 2. Corroborate Prefetch, Amcache, BAM, UserAssist/PCA, 4688/Sysmon and EDR/XDR telemetry.
 3. Examine parent/child, arguments, current directory, adjacent DLLs/configuration and connections.
 4. Do not date the execution using Amcache LinkDate or Shimcache. Examine $MFT/$UsnJrnl for creation/deletion.
 5. Search for the hash and characteristics in approved intelligence sources; interpret the results with caution.
 6. Extend the hunt to the same hash, path, original name, account and process chain; preserve items before remediation.`,command:'',queries:[
  {platform:'Splunk',title:'Name / path / hash in EDR events',query:'index=edr earliest={{START}} latest={{END}} (host="{{HOSTNAME}}" OR dest="{{HOSTNAME}}") ("{{FILENAME}}" OR "{{SHA256}}" OR "{{IOC}}") | table _time host user file_name file_path process_name process_command_line parent_process_name sha256'},
  {platform:'Microsoft Defender',title:'Execution and hash',query:'DeviceProcessEvents\n| where Timestamp between (datetime({{START}}) .. datetime({{END}}))\n| where DeviceName =~ "{{HOSTNAME}}" or FileName =~ "{{FILENAME}}" or SHA256 =~ "{{SHA256}}"\n| project Timestamp, DeviceName, AccountName, FileName, FolderPath, SHA256, ProcessCommandLine, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine\n| order by Timestamp desc'},
  {platform:'Cortex XDR',title:'Execution & rclone / MEGAsync hunt',query:'dataset = xdr_data\n| filter event_type = ENUM.PROCESS\n| filter _time >= to_timestamp("{{START}}") and _time <= to_timestamp("{{END}}")\n| filter agent_hostname ~= "(?i){{HOSTNAME}}"\n| filter action_process_image_name in ("rclone.exe", "MEGAsync.exe") or action_process_image_command_line ~= "(?i)\\s(copy|sync|move)\\s.+\\s\\S+:\\S*"\n| fields _time, agent_hostname, actor_effective_username, action_process_image_name, action_process_image_path, action_process_image_command_line'}
 ]},
 {id:'command-ram',kind:'command',title:'Capture memory (RAM)',category:'Acquisition · Windows',summary:'Volatile acquisition: authorization, destination media and hash are essential.',content:'Use only an approved, signed capture tool per local procedure. Prepare the destination media, verify free space and the UTC time. Capture necessarily alters the system state slightly: record the tool/version, the command, the start/end times and any errors. WinPmem syntax depends on the version; check `--help` on the deployed binary before acquisition. Example CLI form to be validated in a lab.',command:'winpmem_mini_x64.exe --format raw -o "{{OUTPUT_DIR}}\\{{HOSTNAME}}-memory.raw"',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-kape',kind:'command',title:'Triage collection with KAPE',category:'Acquisition · Windows',summary:'Example collection from a mounted source; adapt targets to the authorized scope.',content:'Run KAPE from an analysis workstation, document the version and verify the installed targets. Use a dedicated, protected destination; the content may include sensitive data. Verify the output and compute hashes after collection.',command:'kape.exe --tsource C: --tdest D:\\case\\tout --target KapeTriage --mdest D:\\case\\mout --module !EZParser',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-prefetch',kind:'command',title:'Parse Prefetch',category:'Analysis · Windows Artifacts',summary:'Analyze a copy of the Prefetch files with PECmd.',content:'The result depends on the OS version, the configuration and the availability of files. Include the source files and acquisition logs in the chain of evidence. Use a dedicated output directory.',command:'PECmd.exe -d "D:\\case\\evidence\\Windows\\Prefetch" --csv D:\\case\\out --csvf prefetch.csv',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-amcache',kind:'command',title:'Parse Amcache',category:'Analysis · Windows Artifacts',summary:'Parse the copied Amcache hive and keep the associated transaction logs.',content:'Collect Amcache.hve with LOG1/LOG2. Run on a working copy. LinkDate is a PE date, not an execution date. The parser and its options may vary depending on the installed version.',command:'AmcacheParser.exe -f "D:\\case\\evidence\\Amcache.hve" -i --csv D:\\case\\out',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-appcompat',kind:'command',title:'Parse AppCompatCache (Shimcache)',category:'Analysis · Windows Artifacts',summary:'Extract AppCompatCache from the copied SYSTEM hive.',content:'Shimcache indicates presence, not proof of execution on Windows 10/11. Its state may reflect the last shutdown: preserve RAM and do not reboot to force a flush. Verify the option against the installed parser version.',command:'AppCompatCacheParser.exe -f "D:\\case\\evidence\\SYSTEM" --csv D:\\case\\out',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-recmd',kind:'command',title:'Parse hives with RECmd',category:'Analysis · Windows Registry',summary:'Example of batch processing with a RECmd batch file from an evidence copy.',content:'Verify the batch file and hive paths for the version used. Archive the parameters, logs and results; parse working copies only.',command:'RECmd.exe -d "D:\\case\\evidence\\C" --bn BatchExamples\\Kroll_Batch.reb --csv D:\\case\\out',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-srum',kind:'command',title:'Parse SRUM',category:'Analysis · Windows Artifacts',summary:'Pair the SRUM database with the SOFTWARE hive from the same evidence set.',content:'Work on verified copies of SRUDB.dat and SOFTWARE. SRUM does not identify the network destination; cross-reference with network logs. Never repair the original database.',command:'SrumECmd.exe -f "D:\\case\\evidence\\SRUDB.dat" -r "D:\\case\\evidence\\SOFTWARE" --csv D:\\case\\out',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-hash',kind:'command',title:'SHA-256 hash of items',category:'Preservation · Windows',summary:'Compute and record a hash before analysis or after acquisition.',content:'Keep the output, path, timestamp and operator identity in the evidence log. Recompute the hash of the copy during transfer verifications.',command:'Get-FileHash -LiteralPath "D:\\case\\evidence\\{{FILENAME}}" -Algorithm SHA256 | Format-List',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-events',kind:'command',title:'Export Windows event logs',category:'Acquisition · Logs',summary:'Export EVTX channels for offline analysis; the list must match the scope.',content:'Record the collection times and any missing or inaccessible channels. Export to dedicated evidence media. Verify that clocks and time zone are documented.',command:'wevtutil epl Security "D:\\case\\evidence\\{{HOSTNAME}}-Security.evtx" /ow:true\nwevtutil epl System "D:\\case\\evidence\\{{HOSTNAME}}-System.evtx" /ow:true',queries:[]},
 {id:'playbook-credential-theft',kind:'playbook',title:'Credential Theft & LSASS Dumping',category:'Windows · Credential Access',summary:'Detect and scope credential dumping, then contain every account that may have been exposed.',content:`1. Preserve EDR telemetry, Sysmon (EID 10 on lsass.exe), Security 4688/4656/4663 and any dump files before cleanup.
2. Identify the process accessing LSASS (comsvcs.dll MiniDump, procdump, Task Manager, Mimikatz, custom tools), its parent, user and command line.
3. Locate dump files or exported hives (SAM, SYSTEM, SECURITY, NTDS.dit); hash and secure them as sensitive evidence.
4. List every account that had an active session or cached credentials on the host; treat them as exposed.
5. Hunt for the same tool, hash and command line on other hosts, then look for subsequent logons with the exposed accounts.
6. Reset exposed credentials in the agreed order (service accounts, admins, users), rotate krbtgt when domain-wide exposure is confirmed, and monitor for reuse.`,command:'',queries:[
  {platform:'Splunk',title:'LSASS dumping indicators',query:'index=edr earliest={{START}} latest={{END}} host="{{HOSTNAME}}" (process_command_line="*comsvcs*MiniDump*" OR process_command_line="*procdump*lsass*" OR process_command_line="*sekurlsa*" OR process_name="mimikatz.exe") | table _time host user process_name process_command_line parent_process_name'},
  {platform:'Microsoft Defender',title:'LSASS access and dump tooling',query:'DeviceProcessEvents\n| where Timestamp between (datetime({{START}}) .. datetime({{END}}))\n| where DeviceName =~ "{{HOSTNAME}}"\n| where ProcessCommandLine has_any ("comsvcs", "MiniDump", "sekurlsa", "procdump", "lsass")\n| project Timestamp, DeviceName, AccountName, FileName, ProcessCommandLine, InitiatingProcessFileName\n| order by Timestamp desc'},
  {platform:'Cortex XDR',title:'Credential dumping command lines',query:'dataset = xdr_data\n| filter event_type = ENUM.PROCESS\n| filter _time >= to_timestamp("{{START}}") and _time <= to_timestamp("{{END}}")\n| filter agent_hostname ~= "(?i){{HOSTNAME}}"\n| filter action_process_image_command_line ~= "(?i)(comsvcs.*minidump|sekurlsa|procdump.*lsass|ntdsutil)"\n| fields _time, agent_hostname, actor_effective_username, action_process_image_name, action_process_image_command_line'}
 ]},
 {id:'playbook-lateral-movement',kind:'playbook',title:'Lateral Movement (RDP, SMB, WMI, PsExec)',category:'Windows · Lateral Movement',summary:'Reconstruct how an intruder moved between hosts and which accounts and protocols were used.',content:`1. Preserve Security 4624/4625/4648/4672/4776, RDP (TerminalServices) logs, Sysmon, firewall and EDR data from source and destination hosts.
2. Build a hop graph: source host, destination host, account, logon type, protocol and time for each connection.
3. Look for remote execution artifacts: PsExec/PSEXESVC service (7045), WMI process creation (WmiPrvSE), WinRM (wsmprovhost), scheduled tasks, SMB admin shares.
4. Check for RDP tunnelling, new local administrators and first-seen logons for the account.
5. Identify patient zero and the earliest hop; compare against normal administrative behaviour with the client.
6. Contain the compromised accounts and hosts, close the used paths (SMB/RDP/WinRM segmentation) and keep monitoring for new hops.`,command:'',queries:[
  {platform:'Splunk',title:'Remote logons for an account or host',query:'index=wineventlog earliest={{START}} latest={{END}} (EventCode=4624 OR EventCode=4648 OR EventCode=4672) (Account_Name="{{EMAIL}}" OR host="{{HOSTNAME}}") | table _time host EventCode Logon_Type Account_Name Source_Network_Address Process_Name'},
  {platform:'Microsoft Defender',title:'Remote and network logons',query:'DeviceLogonEvents\n| where Timestamp between (datetime({{START}}) .. datetime({{END}}))\n| where LogonType in ("RemoteInteractive", "Network", "NewCredentials")\n| where DeviceName =~ "{{HOSTNAME}}" or AccountName =~ "{{EMAIL}}" or RemoteIP == "{{IP}}"\n| project Timestamp, DeviceName, AccountName, LogonType, RemoteDeviceName, RemoteIP, ActionType\n| order by Timestamp desc'},
  {platform:'Cortex XDR',title:'Remote execution tools',query:'dataset = xdr_data\n| filter event_type = ENUM.PROCESS\n| filter _time >= to_timestamp("{{START}}") and _time <= to_timestamp("{{END}}")\n| filter agent_hostname ~= "(?i){{HOSTNAME}}"\n| filter action_process_image_name in ("psexec.exe", "PSEXESVC.exe", "wmic.exe", "winrs.exe") or actor_process_image_name = "WmiPrvSE.exe"\n| fields _time, agent_hostname, actor_effective_username, action_process_image_name, action_process_image_command_line, actor_process_image_name'}
 ]},
 {id:'playbook-persistence',kind:'playbook',title:'Persistence Mechanisms',category:'Windows · Persistence',summary:'Find and document run keys, services, scheduled tasks, WMI subscriptions and other autostart locations.',content:`1. Enumerate autostart locations on the host with Autoruns or Velociraptor and export the raw output as evidence.
2. Review Run/RunOnce keys, Services (7045/4697), Scheduled Tasks (4698/106), Startup folders, WMI event subscriptions, IFEO/AppInit, COM hijacks and Office add-ins.
3. For each suspicious entry, record the creation time, the creating process and the account; verify the signer and hash of the target.
4. Establish whether the entry was created by the intruder or by legitimate software; compare with a clean baseline.
5. Search all hosts for the same entry name, path and hash.
6. Remove persistence only after preservation and scoping are complete, then validate that it does not return.`,command:'',queries:[
  {platform:'Splunk',title:'Task, service and Run key creation',query:'index=edr earliest={{START}} latest={{END}} host="{{HOSTNAME}}" (process_command_line="*schtasks*/create*" OR process_command_line="*sc*create*" OR process_command_line="*CurrentVersion*Run*") | table _time host user process_name process_command_line parent_process_name'},
  {platform:'Microsoft Defender',title:'Autostart registry modifications',query:'DeviceRegistryEvents\n| where Timestamp between (datetime({{START}}) .. datetime({{END}}))\n| where DeviceName =~ "{{HOSTNAME}}"\n| where RegistryKey has_any ("CurrentVersion\\\\Run", "CurrentVersion\\\\RunOnce", "Winlogon", "Image File Execution Options")\n| project Timestamp, DeviceName, ActionType, RegistryKey, RegistryValueName, RegistryValueData, InitiatingProcessFileName\n| order by Timestamp desc'},
  {platform:'Cortex XDR',title:'Scheduled tasks and services',query:'dataset = xdr_data\n| filter event_type = ENUM.PROCESS\n| filter _time >= to_timestamp("{{START}}") and _time <= to_timestamp("{{END}}")\n| filter agent_hostname ~= "(?i){{HOSTNAME}}"\n| filter action_process_image_command_line ~= "(?i)(schtasks.*/create|sc(\\.exe)?\\s+create|New-Service|Register-ScheduledTask)"\n| fields _time, agent_hostname, actor_effective_username, action_process_image_name, action_process_image_command_line'}
 ]},
 {id:'playbook-phishing',kind:'playbook',title:'Phishing & Malicious Attachment',category:'Email · Initial Access',summary:'Scope a phishing campaign, identify who interacted with it and what executed.',content:`1. Collect the original message (EML/MSG with full headers), attachment, URLs and the reporter details; do not forward live samples.
2. Search the mail platform for the same sender, subject, URL, attachment hash and sending infrastructure to find every recipient.
3. Determine who opened, clicked or replied, using mail, proxy and endpoint telemetry.
4. Detonate or analyse the attachment/URL in an isolated sandbox; extract IOCs and record the tool and version.
5. Check endpoints of clickers for child processes of the mail client or Office, downloads and credential entry.
6. Purge the messages, block sender/URL/hash, reset the credentials of affected users and brief the client.`,command:'',queries:[
  {platform:'Splunk',title:'Messages from a sender or with a URL',query:'index=email earliest={{START}} latest={{END}} (sender="{{EMAIL}}" OR recipient="{{EMAIL}}" OR url="*{{IOC}}*" OR file_hash="{{SHA256}}") | table _time sender recipient subject action url file_name file_hash'},
  {platform:'Microsoft Defender',title:'Email, URL and attachment correlation',query:'EmailEvents\n| where Timestamp between (datetime({{START}}) .. datetime({{END}}))\n| where SenderFromAddress =~ "{{EMAIL}}" or RecipientEmailAddress =~ "{{EMAIL}}"\n| join kind=leftouter (EmailAttachmentInfo | project NetworkMessageId, FileName, SHA256) on NetworkMessageId\n| project Timestamp, SenderFromAddress, RecipientEmailAddress, Subject, DeliveryAction, FileName, SHA256\n| order by Timestamp desc'},
  {platform:'Cortex XDR',title:'Office and mail client child processes',query:'dataset = xdr_data\n| filter event_type = ENUM.PROCESS\n| filter _time >= to_timestamp("{{START}}") and _time <= to_timestamp("{{END}}")\n| filter agent_hostname ~= "(?i){{HOSTNAME}}"\n| filter actor_process_image_name in ("WINWORD.EXE", "EXCEL.EXE", "OUTLOOK.EXE", "POWERPNT.EXE")\n| filter action_process_image_name in ("cmd.exe", "powershell.exe", "wscript.exe", "mshta.exe", "rundll32.exe")\n| fields _time, agent_hostname, actor_effective_username, actor_process_image_name, action_process_image_name, action_process_image_command_line'}
 ]},
 {id:'playbook-webshell',kind:'playbook',title:'Web Server Compromise & Webshell',category:'Server · Initial Access',summary:'Confirm exploitation of an exposed application, find the webshell and scope post-exploitation.',content:`1. Preserve web server, WAF, reverse proxy and application logs plus a forensic copy of the web root before any change.
2. Identify the exploited vulnerability window: first suspicious request, source IPs, user agents and HTTP status codes.
3. Find the webshell by file creation time, unusual script files in web directories and requests that return 200 to rarely used paths.
4. Review child processes of the web server (w3wp.exe, httpd, nginx, tomcat, php-fpm) for cmd, PowerShell, whoami, curl and wget.
5. Determine what the shell did: reconnaissance, credential access, tunnelling, data access or lateral movement.
6. Patch or isolate the application, remove the shell after preservation, rotate secrets stored on the server and monitor for reinfection.`,command:'',queries:[
  {platform:'Splunk',title:'Suspicious requests to the web server',query:'index=web earliest={{START}} latest={{END}} (host="{{HOSTNAME}}" OR src_ip="{{IP}}") (uri_path="*.aspx" OR uri_path="*.jsp" OR uri_path="*.php") status=200 | stats count values(src_ip) as sources values(http_user_agent) as agents by uri_path | sort - count'},
  {platform:'Microsoft Defender',title:'Shells spawned by web server processes',query:'DeviceProcessEvents\n| where Timestamp between (datetime({{START}}) .. datetime({{END}}))\n| where DeviceName =~ "{{HOSTNAME}}"\n| where InitiatingProcessFileName in~ ("w3wp.exe", "httpd.exe", "nginx.exe", "php-cgi.exe", "tomcat9.exe")\n| where FileName in~ ("cmd.exe", "powershell.exe", "whoami.exe", "certutil.exe", "net.exe")\n| project Timestamp, DeviceName, FileName, ProcessCommandLine, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine\n| order by Timestamp desc'},
  {platform:'Cortex XDR',title:'Web server process children',query:'dataset = xdr_data\n| filter event_type = ENUM.PROCESS\n| filter _time >= to_timestamp("{{START}}") and _time <= to_timestamp("{{END}}")\n| filter agent_hostname ~= "(?i){{HOSTNAME}}"\n| filter actor_process_image_name in ("w3wp.exe", "httpd.exe", "nginx.exe", "tomcat9.exe", "java.exe")\n| filter action_process_image_name in ("cmd.exe", "powershell.exe", "whoami.exe", "certutil.exe")\n| fields _time, agent_hostname, actor_process_image_name, action_process_image_name, action_process_image_command_line'}
 ]},
 {id:'playbook-ad-compromise',kind:'playbook',title:'Active Directory Compromise',category:'Identity · Active Directory',summary:'Detect Kerberoasting, DCSync, NTDS theft and privileged group changes, then plan domain recovery.',content:`1. Preserve Domain Controller Security logs (4624, 4662, 4728/4732/4756, 4768/4769, 4720), directory replication logs and EDR data from DCs.
2. Look for Kerberoasting (4769 with RC4 tickets for service accounts), AS-REP roasting, DCSync (4662 with replication GUIDs from non-DC sources) and NTDS.dit access (ntdsutil, vssadmin, diskshadow).
3. Review changes to Domain Admins, Enterprise Admins, Backup Operators, GPOs, AdminSDHolder and delegation settings.
4. Identify golden/silver ticket indicators: tickets with abnormal lifetime, TGS without TGT, unknown service accounts.
5. Decide the recovery strategy with the client: privileged account reset, double krbtgt reset, rebuild of compromised DCs and tiering review.
6. Monitor identity telemetry closely after remediation for reuse of old tickets or hashes.`,command:'',queries:[
  {platform:'Splunk',title:'Kerberoasting and DCSync indicators',query:'index=wineventlog earliest={{START}} latest={{END}} ((EventCode=4769 Ticket_Encryption_Type=0x17) OR (EventCode=4662 Properties="*1131f6aa-9c07-11d1-f79f-00c04fc2dcd2*")) | table _time host EventCode Account_Name Service_Name Client_Address'},
  {platform:'Microsoft Defender',title:'Identity directory events',query:'IdentityDirectoryEvents\n| where Timestamp between (datetime({{START}}) .. datetime({{END}}))\n| where AccountName =~ "{{EMAIL}}" or TargetAccountUpn =~ "{{EMAIL}}" or DestinationDeviceName =~ "{{HOSTNAME}}"\n| project Timestamp, ActionType, AccountName, TargetAccountUpn, DestinationDeviceName, AdditionalFields\n| order by Timestamp desc'},
  {platform:'Cortex XDR',title:'AD attack tooling',query:'dataset = xdr_data\n| filter event_type = ENUM.PROCESS\n| filter _time >= to_timestamp("{{START}}") and _time <= to_timestamp("{{END}}")\n| filter action_process_image_command_line ~= "(?i)(lsadump|dcsync|kerberoast|rubeus|ntdsutil|vssadmin.*create.*shadow|diskshadow)"\n| fields _time, agent_hostname, actor_effective_username, action_process_image_name, action_process_image_command_line'}
 ]},
 {id:'playbook-c2-beaconing',kind:'playbook',title:'C2 Beaconing & DNS Tunnelling',category:'Network · Command and Control',summary:'Identify periodic callbacks and abnormal DNS patterns and link them to a process on the host.',content:`1. Define the suspect host or destination and preserve DNS, proxy, firewall, NetFlow and EDR network telemetry for the period.
2. Look for regular connection intervals, similar byte sizes, rare destinations, new domains, long or high-entropy DNS names and unusual TXT queries.
3. Attribute the traffic to a process, user and parent chain using EDR; confirm with proxy or firewall logs.
4. Enrich the destination (registration age, hosting, certificates, JA3/JA3S) and compare with approved intelligence sources without leaking case data.
5. Search the estate for other hosts contacting the same destination and for the same process or beacon configuration.
6. Block confirmed infrastructure, isolate affected hosts per the incident lead and preserve memory for configuration extraction.`,command:'',queries:[
  {platform:'Splunk',title:'Connection frequency per destination',query:'index=proxy OR index=firewall earliest={{START}} latest={{END}} (src_ip="{{IP}}" OR dest_ip="{{IP}}" OR host="{{HOSTNAME}}") | bin _time span=10m | stats count by src_ip dest_ip dest_port _time | stats count as buckets sum(count) as total by src_ip dest_ip dest_port | where buckets>20 | sort - total'},
  {platform:'Microsoft Defender',title:'Repeated outbound connections',query:'DeviceNetworkEvents\n| where Timestamp between (datetime({{START}}) .. datetime({{END}}))\n| where DeviceName =~ "{{HOSTNAME}}" or RemoteIP == "{{IP}}"\n| summarize Connections=count(), FirstSeen=min(Timestamp), LastSeen=max(Timestamp) by DeviceName, InitiatingProcessFileName, RemoteIP, RemotePort\n| order by Connections desc'},
  {platform:'Cortex XDR',title:'Top destinations by process',query:'dataset = xdr_data\n| filter event_type = ENUM.NETWORK\n| filter _time >= to_timestamp("{{START}}") and _time <= to_timestamp("{{END}}")\n| filter agent_hostname ~= "(?i){{HOSTNAME}}" or action_remote_ip ~= "{{IP}}"\n| comp count() as connections by agent_hostname, actor_process_image_name, action_remote_ip, action_remote_port\n| sort desc connections'}
 ]},
 {id:'playbook-defense-evasion',kind:'playbook',title:'Defense Evasion & Log Tampering',category:'Windows · Defense Evasion',summary:'Detect disabled security tools, cleared logs and tampering with backups and shadow copies.',content:`1. Preserve the remaining logs immediately; export Security, System, PowerShell and Sysmon channels and EDR agent health data.
2. Look for log clearing (1102, 104), audit policy changes (4719), Defender exclusions or disablement, stopped security services and EDR tamper alerts.
3. Check for shadow copy and backup deletion (vssadmin, wbadmin, bcdedit, wmic shadowcopy) which often precede ransomware.
4. Identify the account, process and time of each action and correlate with the earlier intrusion timeline.
5. Verify the integrity and coverage of remaining telemetry; document the visibility gaps in the report.
6. Restore security tooling, review exclusions and protect backups with immutable copies.`,command:'',queries:[
  {platform:'Splunk',title:'Log clearing and policy changes',query:'index=wineventlog earliest={{START}} latest={{END}} host="{{HOSTNAME}}" (EventCode=1102 OR EventCode=104 OR EventCode=4719 OR EventCode=7036) | table _time host EventCode Account_Name Message'},
  {platform:'Microsoft Defender',title:'Security tooling tampering',query:'DeviceProcessEvents\n| where Timestamp between (datetime({{START}}) .. datetime({{END}}))\n| where DeviceName =~ "{{HOSTNAME}}"\n| where ProcessCommandLine has_any ("Set-MpPreference", "DisableRealtimeMonitoring", "wevtutil cl", "vssadmin delete", "bcdedit", "wbadmin delete", "sc stop")\n| project Timestamp, DeviceName, AccountName, FileName, ProcessCommandLine\n| order by Timestamp desc'},
  {platform:'Cortex XDR',title:'Backup and log destruction commands',query:'dataset = xdr_data\n| filter event_type = ENUM.PROCESS\n| filter _time >= to_timestamp("{{START}}") and _time <= to_timestamp("{{END}}")\n| filter agent_hostname ~= "(?i){{HOSTNAME}}"\n| filter action_process_image_command_line ~= "(?i)(vssadmin.*delete|wbadmin.*delete|wevtutil.*\\scl|bcdedit.*recoveryenabled|Set-MpPreference)"\n| fields _time, agent_hostname, actor_effective_username, action_process_image_name, action_process_image_command_line'}
 ]},
 {id:'playbook-lolbins',kind:'playbook',title:'Malicious PowerShell & LOLBins',category:'Windows · Execution',summary:'Hunt living-off-the-land abuse: encoded PowerShell, certutil, mshta, rundll32, regsvr32 and bitsadmin.',content:`1. Preserve PowerShell logs (4103/4104), Sysmon EID 1 and EDR process telemetry; enable Script Block Logging if absent and note the limitation.
2. Decode encoded commands and record the full decoded content with the tool used; treat the result as sensitive.
3. Identify the parent process, user, destination URL/IP and any dropped files or loaded modules.
4. Distinguish legitimate administration (software deployment, management tools) from abuse by comparing with the client baseline.
5. Hunt for the same commands, URLs and hashes across the estate and review related persistence.
6. Add detections or constrained language mode where appropriate and document false positives.`,command:'',queries:[
  {platform:'Splunk',title:'Encoded and download cradle commands',query:'index=edr earliest={{START}} latest={{END}} host="{{HOSTNAME}}" (process_command_line="*-enc*" OR process_command_line="*DownloadString*" OR process_command_line="*FromBase64String*" OR process_command_line="*certutil*urlcache*" OR process_name="mshta.exe" OR process_name="regsvr32.exe") | table _time host user process_name process_command_line parent_process_name'},
  {platform:'Microsoft Defender',title:'LOLBin abuse',query:'DeviceProcessEvents\n| where Timestamp between (datetime({{START}}) .. datetime({{END}}))\n| where DeviceName =~ "{{HOSTNAME}}"\n| where FileName in~ ("powershell.exe", "pwsh.exe", "certutil.exe", "mshta.exe", "regsvr32.exe", "rundll32.exe", "bitsadmin.exe")\n| where ProcessCommandLine has_any ("-enc", "-EncodedCommand", "DownloadString", "urlcache", "http", "scrobj", "FromBase64String")\n| project Timestamp, DeviceName, AccountName, FileName, ProcessCommandLine, InitiatingProcessFileName\n| order by Timestamp desc'},
  {platform:'Cortex XDR',title:'LOLBin command lines',query:'dataset = xdr_data\n| filter event_type = ENUM.PROCESS\n| filter _time >= to_timestamp("{{START}}") and _time <= to_timestamp("{{END}}")\n| filter agent_hostname ~= "(?i){{HOSTNAME}}"\n| filter action_process_image_command_line ~= "(?i)(-enc|-encodedcommand|downloadstring|frombase64string|urlcache|bitsadmin.*transfer|scrobj)"\n| fields _time, agent_hostname, actor_effective_username, action_process_image_name, action_process_image_command_line'}
 ]},
 {id:'playbook-cloud-identity',kind:'playbook',title:'Cloud Account Compromise (Microsoft 365 / Entra ID)',category:'Cloud · Identity',summary:'Investigate suspicious sign-ins, OAuth consent grants, token theft and mailbox or role persistence.',content:`1. Preserve Entra ID sign-in and audit logs, the Unified Audit Log and mailbox audit data; extend retention if the licence allows.
2. Review sign-ins for the account: new IPs and countries, impossible travel, legacy protocols, MFA prompts, device and user agent, and token or session reuse.
3. Examine OAuth app consents, service principal credentials, new app registrations, role assignments, conditional access changes and MFA method changes.
4. Check mailbox rules, forwarding, delegations and mail flow rules created during the exposure window.
5. Revoke refresh tokens and sessions, reset the password, remove malicious apps and rogue authentication methods, and re-enforce MFA.
6. Quantify accessed data and notify the client according to the agreed process and applicable obligations.`,command:'',queries:[
  {platform:'Splunk',title:'Entra ID sign-ins for an account',query:'index=azure sourcetype=azure:aad:signin earliest={{START}} latest={{END}} (userPrincipalName="{{EMAIL}}" OR ipAddress="{{IP}}") | table _time userPrincipalName ipAddress location.countryOrRegion appDisplayName clientAppUsed status.errorCode deviceDetail.operatingSystem'},
  {platform:'Microsoft Defender',title:'Consent grants and rule changes',query:'CloudAppEvents\n| where Timestamp between (datetime({{START}}) .. datetime({{END}}))\n| where AccountId =~ "{{EMAIL}}" or AccountDisplayName =~ "{{EMAIL}}"\n| where ActionType has_any ("Consent to application", "Add service principal", "New-InboxRule", "Set-InboxRule", "Add member to role", "Update user")\n| project Timestamp, AccountId, ActionType, IPAddress, UserAgent, Application, RawEventData\n| order by Timestamp desc'}
 ]},
 {id:'playbook-insider-data-theft',kind:'playbook',title:'Insider Data Theft & Removable Media',category:'Insider Threat · Data Loss',summary:'Document data movement to USB, personal cloud and email with legal and HR coordination.',content:`1. Involve legal, HR and the client contact before any collection; define scope, authority and confidentiality rules in writing.
2. Preserve endpoint, proxy, DLP, email and cloud audit logs for the user and device; image the device only with authorisation.
3. Establish what was accessed and copied: file access auditing, USB insertion events (USBSTOR, setupapi), LNK/Jump Lists, cloud sync clients and web uploads.
4. Build a factual timeline linking the account, device, files and destinations; avoid conclusions about intent.
5. Restrict the account access in coordination with HR and preserve the mailbox and cloud storage with legal hold where needed.
6. Deliver an evidence-based report limited to observed facts, with confidence levels and limitations.`,command:'',queries:[
  {platform:'Splunk',title:'USB and file copy activity',query:'index=edr earliest={{START}} latest={{END}} (user="{{EMAIL}}" OR host="{{HOSTNAME}}") (event_type="usb_mount" OR event_type="file_copy" OR file_path="E:*" OR file_path="F:*") | table _time host user event_type file_name file_path file_size'},
  {platform:'Microsoft Defender',title:'Removable media and file activity',query:'DeviceEvents\n| where Timestamp between (datetime({{START}}) .. datetime({{END}}))\n| where DeviceName =~ "{{HOSTNAME}}"\n| where ActionType in ("UsbDriveMounted", "UsbDriveUnmounted", "PnpDeviceConnected")\n| project Timestamp, DeviceName, ActionType, AdditionalFields\n| order by Timestamp desc'},
  {platform:'Cortex XDR',title:'File writes to removable drives',query:'dataset = xdr_data\n| filter event_type = ENUM.FILE\n| filter _time >= to_timestamp("{{START}}") and _time <= to_timestamp("{{END}}")\n| filter agent_hostname ~= "(?i){{HOSTNAME}}" and action_file_path ~= "(?i)^[d-z]:"\n| fields _time, agent_hostname, actor_effective_username, action_file_path, action_file_name, action_file_size'}
 ]},
 {id:'command-velociraptor',kind:'command',title:'Collect triage with Velociraptor',category:'Acquisition · Windows',summary:'Run a triage collection from a standalone Velociraptor binary into a ZIP.',content:'Use only an authorised binary and a protected destination. Verify the artifact name and parameters for the deployed version, run a limited collection first and hash the resulting archive.',command:'velociraptor.exe artifacts collect Windows.KapeFiles.Targets --args _SANS_Triage=Y --output "{{OUTPUT_DIR}}\\{{HOSTNAME}}-triage.zip"',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-disk-image',kind:'command',title:'Image a disk with FTK Imager CLI',category:'Acquisition · Windows',summary:'Create a verified E01 image of a physical drive.',content:'Use write-blocking where possible, a destination with enough space and an approved tool version. Record the drive serial number, tool version, hash results and the operator.',command:'ftkimager.exe \\\\.\\PhysicalDrive0 "{{OUTPUT_DIR}}\\{{HOSTNAME}}-disk" --e01 --frag 2G --compress 6 --verify',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-linux-dd',kind:'command',title:'Image a Linux disk with dd',category:'Acquisition · Linux',summary:'Create a raw image of a block device and compute its hash.',content:'Identify the device carefully (lsblk) and write only to dedicated evidence media. Prefer a write blocker for offline disks. Record the hash of the source and the image.',command:'sudo dd if=/dev/sda of=/mnt/evidence/{{HOSTNAME}}.dd bs=4M conv=noerror,sync status=progress\nsha256sum /mnt/evidence/{{HOSTNAME}}.dd',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-lime',kind:'command',title:'Capture Linux memory with LiME',category:'Acquisition · Linux',summary:'Load the LiME kernel module to dump memory.',content:'The module must match the running kernel. Capture alters the system slightly; record the kernel version, module hash and the command used. Store the output on external media.',command:'sudo insmod lime.ko "path=/mnt/evidence/{{HOSTNAME}}.lime format=lime"',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-vol3-pslist',kind:'command',title:'Volatility 3: process list and tree',category:'Memory Analysis',summary:'List processes and parent relationships from a memory image.',content:'Use the symbol tables that match the OS build. Compare pslist, psscan and pstree to spot hidden or terminated processes. Work on a copy of the image.',command:'vol -f "{{OUTPUT_DIR}}\\{{HOSTNAME}}-memory.raw" windows.pstree.PsTree',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-vol3-cmdline',kind:'command',title:'Volatility 3: command lines',category:'Memory Analysis',summary:'Extract the command line of each process in the image.',content:'Command lines can reveal encoded commands, staging paths and tool arguments. Cross-check with EDR and 4688/Sysmon data.',command:'vol -f "{{OUTPUT_DIR}}\\{{HOSTNAME}}-memory.raw" windows.cmdline.CmdLine',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-vol3-netscan',kind:'command',title:'Volatility 3: network connections',category:'Memory Analysis',summary:'List network sockets and connections found in memory.',content:'Correlate remote addresses with proxy and firewall logs. Memory may hold closed connections that are no longer visible on the live host.',command:'vol -f "{{OUTPUT_DIR}}\\{{HOSTNAME}}-memory.raw" windows.netscan.NetScan',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-vol3-malfind',kind:'command',title:'Volatility 3: injected code (malfind)',category:'Memory Analysis',summary:'Find suspicious executable memory regions that may indicate injection.',content:'Results include false positives. Dump candidate regions to a protected directory, hash them and analyse in an isolated environment.',command:'vol -f "{{OUTPUT_DIR}}\\{{HOSTNAME}}-memory.raw" -o "{{OUTPUT_DIR}}\\malfind" windows.malfind.Malfind --dump',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-mftecmd',kind:'command',title:'Parse $MFT with MFTECmd',category:'Analysis · File System',summary:'Convert the Master File Table to CSV for timeline analysis.',content:'Work on a verified copy of $MFT. Compare $STANDARD_INFORMATION and $FILE_NAME timestamps to spot timestomping. Document the tool version.',command:'MFTECmd.exe -f "D:\\case\\evidence\\$MFT" --csv "D:\\case\\out" --csvf mft.csv',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-usnjrnl',kind:'command',title:'Parse $UsnJrnl with MFTECmd',category:'Analysis · File System',summary:'Parse the change journal to see file creation, rename and deletion.',content:'Use the $J stream together with $MFT for full path resolution. The journal has limited retention, so collect it early.',command:'MFTECmd.exe -f "D:\\case\\evidence\\$J" -m "D:\\case\\evidence\\$MFT" --csv "D:\\case\\out" --csvf usnjrnl.csv',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-evtxecmd',kind:'command',title:'Parse EVTX with EvtxECmd',category:'Analysis · Windows Logs',summary:'Convert a directory of event logs to normalised CSV.',content:'Use the maps shipped with the tool and verify them for the version installed. Keep the original EVTX files read-only.',command:'EvtxECmd.exe -d "D:\\case\\evidence\\Logs" --csv "D:\\case\\out" --csvf events.csv',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-lecmd',kind:'command',title:'Parse LNK files with LECmd',category:'Analysis · Windows Artifacts',summary:'Extract target path, timestamps and volume details from shortcut files.',content:'LNK files can prove that a user opened a file or folder, including on removable media. Record the volume serial number and machine identifier.',command:'LECmd.exe -d "D:\\case\\evidence\\Recent" --csv "D:\\case\\out" --csvf lnk.csv',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-jlecmd',kind:'command',title:'Parse Jump Lists with JLECmd',category:'Analysis · Windows Artifacts',summary:'Extract application usage and recent file history from Jump Lists.',content:'Automatic and custom destinations show files opened per application. Corroborate with LNK, ShellBags and file system artifacts.',command:'JLECmd.exe -d "D:\\case\\evidence\\JumpLists" --csv "D:\\case\\out" --csvf jumplists.csv',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-sbecmd',kind:'command',title:'Parse ShellBags with SBECmd',category:'Analysis · Windows Registry',summary:'Reveal folders browsed by a user, including network and removable locations.',content:'Run on copies of NTUSER.DAT and UsrClass.dat. ShellBags show that a folder was viewed, not that files were copied.',command:'SBECmd.exe -d "D:\\case\\evidence\\Users" --csv "D:\\case\\out"',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-hayabusa',kind:'command',title:'Hayabusa: Sigma-based EVTX timeline',category:'Analysis · Windows Logs',summary:'Generate a detection timeline from event logs.',content:'Update the rules before use and record their version. Review high and critical detections first, then validate each one against the raw event.',command:'hayabusa.exe csv-timeline -d "D:\\case\\evidence\\Logs" -o "D:\\case\\out\\hayabusa.csv" -p verbose',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-chainsaw',kind:'command',title:'Chainsaw: hunt EVTX with Sigma',category:'Analysis · Windows Logs',summary:'Run Sigma rules and built-in detections against event logs.',content:'Provide the matching Sigma rules and mapping file for the installed version. Keep the output with the tool and rule versions.',command:'chainsaw.exe hunt "D:\\case\\evidence\\Logs" -s sigma/ --mapping mappings/sigma-event-logs-all.yml -o "D:\\case\\out\\chainsaw"',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-plaso',kind:'command',title:'Build a Plaso super timeline',category:'Analysis · Timeline',summary:'Create a Plaso storage file and export a CSV timeline.',content:'Super timelines are large; filter by time window after creation. Document the parsers, time zone and tool version used.',command:'log2timeline.py --storage-file "D:\\case\\out\\{{HOSTNAME}}.plaso" "D:\\case\\evidence\\image.E01"\npsort.py -o l2tcsv -w "D:\\case\\out\\{{HOSTNAME}}-timeline.csv" "D:\\case\\out\\{{HOSTNAME}}.plaso" "date > {{START}} AND date < {{END}}"',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-yara',kind:'command',title:'Scan a directory with YARA',category:'Analysis · Malware',summary:'Scan files with a reviewed rule set and show matching strings.',content:'Use rules from a trusted, versioned source and test them for false positives. A match is a lead, not a conclusion.',command:'yara64.exe -r -s "D:\\case\\rules\\index.yar" "D:\\case\\evidence\\files"',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-capa',kind:'command',title:'Identify capabilities with capa',category:'Analysis · Malware',summary:'Map the capabilities of an executable to ATT&CK techniques.',content:'Run only in an isolated analysis environment. capa describes potential capabilities, not confirmed behaviour.',command:'capa.exe -vv "D:\\case\\evidence\\{{FILENAME}}"',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-autoruns',kind:'command',title:'Export autostart entries with Autorunsc',category:'Live Response · Persistence',summary:'List autostart locations with hashes and signature verification.',content:'Review unsigned or unusual entries first. Save the CSV output as evidence and compare with a baseline when available.',command:'autorunsc64.exe -accepteula -a * -c -h -s -nobanner > "{{OUTPUT_DIR}}\\{{HOSTNAME}}-autoruns.csv"',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-processes',kind:'command',title:'Running processes with command lines',category:'Live Response · Windows',summary:'List processes with parent, path and command line.',content:'Run after memory capture. Output reflects the current state only, so record the time in UTC.',command:'Get-CimInstance Win32_Process | Select-Object ProcessId,ParentProcessId,Name,ExecutablePath,CommandLine,CreationDate | Export-Csv "{{OUTPUT_DIR}}\\{{HOSTNAME}}-processes.csv" -NoTypeInformation',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-netstat',kind:'command',title:'Network connections with owning process',category:'Live Response · Windows',summary:'List active TCP connections and the processes that own them.',content:'Run after memory capture and map each PID to the process list. Record the time, as connections change quickly.',command:'Get-NetTCPConnection | Select-Object LocalAddress,LocalPort,RemoteAddress,RemotePort,State,OwningProcess | Export-Csv "{{OUTPUT_DIR}}\\{{HOSTNAME}}-connections.csv" -NoTypeInformation',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-schtasks',kind:'command',title:'List scheduled tasks',category:'Live Response · Persistence',summary:'Enumerate scheduled tasks with their actions and last run times.',content:'Look for tasks created recently, running from user-writable paths or launching scripts. Export the XML of suspicious tasks as evidence.',command:'Get-ScheduledTask | ForEach-Object { [pscustomobject]@{Name=$_.TaskName;Path=$_.TaskPath;State=$_.State;Actions=($_.Actions | ForEach-Object { $_.Execute + " " + $_.Arguments }) -join "; "} } | Export-Csv "{{OUTPUT_DIR}}\\{{HOSTNAME}}-tasks.csv" -NoTypeInformation',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-run-keys',kind:'command',title:'Query Run keys',category:'Live Response · Persistence',summary:'Read common autostart registry keys.',content:'Check both HKLM and HKCU, and load offline user hives when investigating other profiles. Compare entries with a baseline.',command:'reg query "HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run"\nreg query "HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\RunOnce"\nreg query "HKCU\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run"',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-services',kind:'command',title:'List services and binary paths',category:'Live Response · Persistence',summary:'Enumerate services with their start mode, account and executable.',content:'Investigate services running from unusual directories or with unusual accounts. Correlate with event 7045 for installation time.',command:'Get-CimInstance Win32_Service | Select-Object Name,DisplayName,State,StartMode,StartName,PathName | Export-Csv "{{OUTPUT_DIR}}\\{{HOSTNAME}}-services.csv" -NoTypeInformation',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-local-admins',kind:'command',title:'Local administrators and sessions',category:'Live Response · Accounts',summary:'List local administrators, logged-on users and SMB sessions.',content:'Unexpected local administrators and sessions are leads, not proof. Record timestamps and compare with the asset inventory.',command:'Get-LocalGroupMember -Group "Administrators"\nquery user\nGet-SmbSession',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-dns-cache',kind:'command',title:'Dump DNS cache',category:'Live Response · Network',summary:'Capture recently resolved names before they expire.',content:'Volatile data: collect early, before rebooting or changing network settings. Correlate with DNS server logs.',command:'Get-DnsClientCache | Select-Object Entry,Name,Data,TimeToLive | Export-Csv "{{OUTPUT_DIR}}\\{{HOSTNAME}}-dnscache.csv" -NoTypeInformation',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-logon-events',kind:'command',title:'Query logon events for an account',category:'Live Response · Windows Logs',summary:'Read recent 4624 events for a specific user.',content:'Requires audit policy to have recorded the events. Check the logon type and source address, and widen the time window as needed.',command:'Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName="Security";Id=4624;StartTime=[datetime]"{{START}}";EndTime=[datetime]"{{END}}"} | Where-Object { $_.Message -match "{{EMAIL}}" } | Select-Object TimeCreated,Id,Message',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-defender-detections',kind:'command',title:'Microsoft Defender detections and exclusions',category:'Live Response · Security Tools',summary:'Review threat detections and configured exclusions.',content:'Exclusions added by an intruder are a strong defense-evasion indicator. Export the output before changing any configuration.',command:'Get-MpThreatDetection | Format-List\nGet-MpPreference | Select-Object ExclusionPath,ExclusionProcess,ExclusionExtension',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-ad-user',kind:'command',title:'Review an Active Directory account',category:'Active Directory',summary:'Show last logon, password age, status and group memberships.',content:'Requires the ActiveDirectory module and read access. Use the exact identity from the case and log the query as part of your notes.',command:'Get-ADUser -Identity "{{USERNAME}}" -Properties LastLogonDate,PasswordLastSet,Enabled,MemberOf | Select-Object Name,Enabled,LastLogonDate,PasswordLastSet,MemberOf',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-ad-privileged',kind:'command',title:'List privileged AD group members',category:'Active Directory',summary:'Enumerate Domain Admins recursively for review.',content:'Compare the result with the approved administrator list from the client. Review recent changes using 4728/4732/4756 events.',command:'Get-ADGroupMember -Identity "Domain Admins" -Recursive | Select-Object Name,SamAccountName,ObjectClass',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-linux-live',kind:'command',title:'Linux live triage basics',category:'Live Response · Linux',summary:'Collect processes, connections, logins and listening services.',content:'Run as root from trusted binaries or a static toolkit on external media. Write output to evidence media, not the local disk, and record the UTC time.',command:'date -u; uname -a; ps auxf; ss -tulpn; last -a | head -50; who -a; systemctl list-units --type=service --state=running',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-linux-persistence',kind:'command',title:'Linux persistence checks',category:'Live Response · Linux',summary:'Review cron, systemd timers, SSH keys and recent changes in common locations.',content:'Compare findings against the known baseline. Preserve files before removing anything.',command:'crontab -l; ls -la /etc/cron* /var/spool/cron; systemctl list-timers --all; find /home /root -name authorized_keys -exec ls -la {} +; find /etc /usr/local/bin -mtime -7 -type f',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-linux-auth',kind:'command',title:'Linux authentication log review',category:'Analysis · Linux Logs',summary:'Search SSH logins and sudo usage in authentication logs.',content:'Log paths depend on the distribution (auth.log on Debian-based, secure on RHEL-based). Also check journald and rotated logs.',command:'grep -E "Accepted|Failed password|sudo:" /var/log/auth.log* | grep -i "{{IP}}"',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-m365-inbox-rules',kind:'command',title:'Exchange Online: inbox rules and forwarding',category:'Cloud · Microsoft 365',summary:'List inbox rules and forwarding settings for a mailbox.',content:'Requires the Exchange Online PowerShell module and appropriate permissions. Export the output before deleting any rule.',command:'Get-InboxRule -Mailbox "{{EMAIL}}" | Format-List Name,Enabled,From,ForwardTo,ForwardAsAttachmentTo,RedirectTo,DeleteMessage\nGet-Mailbox -Identity "{{EMAIL}}" | Format-List ForwardingAddress,ForwardingSmtpAddress,DeliverToMailboxAndForward',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-m365-audit',kind:'command',title:'Search the Unified Audit Log',category:'Cloud · Microsoft 365',summary:'Export audit records for a user in a time window.',content:'Retention depends on the licence. Large results need paging; export to CSV and record the query and operator.',command:'Search-UnifiedAuditLog -StartDate "{{START}}" -EndDate "{{END}}" -UserIds "{{EMAIL}}" -ResultSize 5000 | Export-Csv "{{OUTPUT_DIR}}\\{{EMAIL}}-audit.csv" -NoTypeInformation',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-m365-revoke',kind:'command',title:'Revoke Entra ID sessions',category:'Cloud · Containment',summary:'Revoke refresh tokens and sign-in sessions for a compromised user.',content:'Containment action: confirm authorisation with the incident lead and document the time. Also reset the password and review authentication methods.',command:'Connect-MgGraph -Scopes "User.ReadWrite.All"\nRevoke-MgUserSignInSession -UserId "{{EMAIL}}"',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-m365-auth-methods',kind:'command',title:'List Entra ID authentication methods',category:'Cloud · Identity',summary:'Review MFA and authentication methods registered for a user.',content:'Rogue authentication methods are a common persistence mechanism after account takeover. Export the output as evidence.',command:'Connect-MgGraph -Scopes "UserAuthenticationMethod.Read.All"\nGet-MgUserAuthenticationMethod -UserId "{{EMAIL}}"',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-tcpdump',kind:'command',title:'Capture traffic with tcpdump',category:'Network · Capture',summary:'Capture packets to or from a specific host.',content:'Confirm legal authorisation and scope before capturing traffic. Write the PCAP to evidence media and hash it afterwards.',command:'sudo tcpdump -i any host {{IP}} -w /mnt/evidence/{{HOSTNAME}}-{{IP}}.pcap',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-tshark',kind:'command',title:'Summarise a PCAP with tshark',category:'Network · Analysis',summary:'List conversations and DNS queries from a capture file.',content:'Work on a copy of the capture. Review conversations first, then follow up on unusual destinations and DNS names.',command:'tshark -r "{{OUTPUT_DIR}}\\capture.pcap" -q -z conv,tcp\ntshark -r "{{OUTPUT_DIR}}\\capture.pcap" -Y dns -T fields -e frame.time -e ip.src -e dns.qry.name',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-sha256-linux',kind:'command',title:'SHA-256 hash on Linux',category:'Preservation · Linux',summary:'Compute and record hashes of evidence files.',content:'Store the hash list separately from the evidence and verify it after each transfer.',command:'sha256sum /mnt/evidence/{{FILENAME}} | tee -a /mnt/evidence/hashes.txt',queries:[]},
 {id:'command-certutil-hash',kind:'command',title:'Hash a file with certutil',category:'Preservation · Windows',summary:'Compute a hash on systems where PowerShell is not available.',content:'Useful on restricted hosts. Record the output, path and timestamp in the evidence log.',command:'certutil -hashfile "D:\\case\\evidence\\{{FILENAME}}" SHA256',queries:[]}
];

const blankEditor=(kind:ToolkitKind):EditorState=>({kind,title:'',category:'',summary:'',content:'',command:'',queries:kind==='playbook'?platforms.map(platform=>({platform,title:platform+' search',query:''})):[]});
function isItem(value:unknown):value is ToolkitItem{
 if(!value||typeof value!=='object')return false;
 const v=value as Record<string,unknown>;
 return typeof v.id==='string'&&['guide','playbook','command'].includes(String(v.kind))&&typeof v.title==='string'&&typeof v.category==='string'&&typeof v.summary==='string'&&typeof v.content==='string'&&typeof v.command==='string'&&Array.isArray(v.queries)&&v.queries.every(q=>!!q&&typeof q==='object'&&platforms.includes((q as Query).platform)&&typeof (q as Query).title==='string'&&typeof (q as Query).query==='string');
}
function placeholders(text:string){return [...new Set([...text.matchAll(/\{\{([A-Z0-9_]+)\}\}/g)].map(m=>m[1]))]}
function escapeValue(value:string,platform:Platform){
 const escaped=value.replace(/\\/g,'\\\\').replace(/\r\n|\r|\n/g,'\\n');
 if(platform==='Microsoft Defender')return escaped.replace(/'/g,"\\'").replace(/"/g,'\\"');
 return escaped.replace(/"/g,'\\"');
}
function renderQuery(query:string,values:Record<string,string>,platform:Platform){return query.replace(/\{\{([A-Z0-9_]+)\}\}/g,(_match,key:string)=>escapeValue(values[key]||'',platform))}
function renderCommand(command:string,values:Record<string,string>){return command.replace(/\{\{([A-Z0-9_]+)\}\}/g,(_match,key:string)=>values[key]===undefined?'':values[key].replace(/`/g,'``').replace(/"/g,'`"').replace(/\$/g,'`$').replace(/\r/g,'`r').replace(/\n/g,'`n'))}
function Field({label,children}:{label:string;children:React.ReactNode}){return <div className="dfir-field"><Label>{label}</Label>{children}</div>}
function interpolateText(text:string,values:Record<string,string>,platform:Platform){return renderQuery(text,values,platform)}

export default function DfirToolkit(){
 const [tab,setTab]=useState<ToolkitKind>('guide'),[custom,setCustom]=useState<ToolkitItem[]>([]),[hidden,setHidden]=useState<string[]>([]),[loaded,setLoaded]=useState(false),[storageError,setStorageError]=useState(''),[query,setQuery]=useState(''),[editor,setEditor]=useState<EditorState|null>(null),[editingId,setEditingId]=useState(''),[values,setValues]=useState<Record<string,string>>({});
 useEffect(()=>{
  try{
   const raw=localStorage.getItem(STORAGE_KEY);
   if(raw){const saved:unknown=JSON.parse(raw);if(!saved||typeof saved!=='object'||!Array.isArray((saved as {items?:unknown}).items)||!Array.isArray((saved as {hidden?:unknown}).hidden)||(saved as {items:unknown[]}).items.some(item=>!isItem(item))||(saved as {hidden:unknown[]}).hidden.some(id=>typeof id!=='string'))throw new Error('Saved DFIR entries have an invalid format. Your saved data has not been overwritten.');setCustom((saved as {items:ToolkitItem[]}).items);setHidden((saved as {hidden:string[]}).hidden)}
  }catch(error){setStorageError(error instanceof Error?error.message:'Could not read saved DFIR entries.');}
  setLoaded(true);
 },[]);
 useEffect(()=>{if(!loaded||storageError)return;try{localStorage.setItem(STORAGE_KEY,JSON.stringify({items:custom,hidden}))}catch(error){setStorageError(error instanceof Error?`Could not save DFIR entries in this browser: ${error.message}`:'Could not save DFIR entries in this browser.');}},[loaded,custom,hidden,storageError]);
 const visibleItems=useMemo(()=>[...builtins.filter(item=>!hidden.includes(item.id)),...custom].filter(item=>item.kind===tab&&(!query||[item.title,item.category,item.summary,item.content,item.command,...item.queries.map(q=>q.query)].join(' ').toLowerCase().includes(query.toLowerCase()))),[tab,query,custom,hidden]);
 function openNew(){setEditingId('');setEditor(blankEditor(tab))}
 function openEdit(item:ToolkitItem){setEditingId(item.id);setEditor({...item,queries:item.queries.map(q=>({...q}))})}
 function saveItem(){
  if(!editor)return;
  if(!editor.title.trim()||!editor.summary.trim()||(editor.kind==='command'&&!editor.command.trim())||(editor.kind==='playbook'&&!editor.queries.some(q=>q.query.trim()))||(editor.kind==='guide'&&!editor.content.trim())){toast.error('Complete the title, summary and required content before saving.');return}
  if(editingId)setCustom(items=>items.map(item=>item.id===editingId?{...editor,id:editingId}:item));
  else setCustom(items=>[...items,{...editor,id:crypto.randomUUID()}]);
  setEditor(null);setEditingId('');
 }
 function removeItem(item:ToolkitItem){
  if(!confirm(`Delete “${item.title}”? This action cannot be undone.`))return;
  if(custom.some(entry=>entry.id===item.id))setCustom(items=>items.filter(entry=>entry.id!==item.id));
  else setHidden(ids=>ids.includes(item.id)?ids:[...ids,item.id]);
 }
 async function copy(text:string){
  try{await navigator.clipboard.writeText(text);toast.success('Copied to clipboard.')}
  catch(error){toast.error(error instanceof Error?`Could not access the clipboard: ${error.message}`:'Could not access the clipboard.');}
 }
 function missing(tokens:string[]){return tokens.filter(token=>!values[token]?.trim())}
 const tabs=[{id:'guide' as const,label:'Forensic field guide',icon:BookOpen},{id:'playbook' as const,label:'Attack playbooks',icon:ShieldAlert},{id:'command' as const,label:'Command vault',icon:Terminal}];
 return <section className="dfir-toolkit">
  <div className="page-title"><div><div className="eyebrow">INCIDENT RESPONSE KNOWLEDGE BASE</div><h1>DFIR Fieldbook</h1><p>Forensic notes, investigation playbooks and a reusable command library.</p></div><Badge variant="outline"><HardDrive size={14}/> Browser-local library</Badge></div>
  {storageError&&<div className="error dfir-storage-error" role="alert">{storageError} Personal edits are disabled until this storage issue is resolved.</div>}
  <div className="dfir-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="DFIR resources">{tabs.map(({id,label,icon:Icon})=><button key={id} role="tab" aria-selected={tab===id} className={tab===id?'active':''} onClick={()=>{setTab(id);setQuery('')}}><Icon size={17}/>{label}<span>{[...builtins.filter(i=>!hidden.includes(i.id)),...custom].filter(i=>i.kind===id).length}</span></button>)}</div>
  <div className="dfir-toolbar"><div className="search"><Search size={16}/><Input aria-label="Search this section" placeholder="Search titles, notes, commands…" value={query} onChange={event=>setQuery(event.target.value)}/></div>{hidden.length>0&&<Button variant="outline" onClick={()=>setHidden([])} disabled={!!storageError}><RotateCcw size={16}/>Restore built-ins</Button>}<Button onClick={openNew} disabled={!loaded||!!storageError}><Plus size={16}/>Add {tab==='guide'?'field note':tab==='playbook'?'playbook':'command'}</Button></div>
  {!loaded?<div className="loading">Loading your DFIR library…</div>:visibleItems.length===0?<div className="empty"><BookOpen/><h2>No entries found</h2><p>Try another search or add a resource to this section.</p><Button onClick={openNew} disabled={!!storageError}><Plus size={16}/>Add an entry</Button></div>:<div className="dfir-grid">{visibleItems.map(item=><ResourceCard key={item.id} item={item} editable={custom.some(entry=>entry.id===item.id)} canModify={!storageError} values={values} setValues={setValues} missing={missing} onCopy={copy} onEdit={openEdit} onRemove={removeItem}/>)}</div>}
  <Dialog open={!!editor} onOpenChange={open=>{if(!open){setEditor(null);setEditingId('')}}}><DialogContent className="dfir-editor"><DialogHeader><DialogTitle>{editingId?'Edit resource':'Add a resource'}</DialogTitle><DialogDescription>Resources are saved in this browser only and are not shared with other accounts.</DialogDescription></DialogHeader>
   {editor&&<div className="dfir-editor-fields"><Field label="Title"><Input value={editor.title} onChange={e=>setEditor({...editor,title:e.target.value})} maxLength={120}/></Field><Field label="Category"><Input value={editor.category} onChange={e=>setEditor({...editor,category:e.target.value})} placeholder="e.g. Windows · Identity" maxLength={80}/></Field><Field label="Short description"><Input value={editor.summary} onChange={e=>setEditor({...editor,summary:e.target.value})} maxLength={240}/></Field>
    {editor.kind==='guide'&&<Field label="Forensic note"><Textarea rows={12} value={editor.content} onChange={e=>setEditor({...editor,content:e.target.value})} placeholder="Document artefacts, caveats, collection and corroboration…"/></Field>}
    {editor.kind==='command'&&<><Field label="Usage notes"><Textarea rows={4} value={editor.content} onChange={e=>setEditor({...editor,content:e.target.value})}/></Field><Field label="Command template"><Textarea rows={5} className="dfir-code" value={editor.command} onChange={e=>setEditor({...editor,command:e.target.value})} placeholder={'Use placeholders such as {{HOSTNAME}}, {{IP}}, {{EMAIL}}, {{OUTPUT_DIR}}'}/></Field></>}
    {editor.kind==='playbook'&&<><Field label="Response steps"><Textarea rows={7} value={editor.content} onChange={e=>setEditor({...editor,content:e.target.value})} placeholder="One step per line…"/></Field>{platforms.map((platform,index)=><Field key={platform} label={`${platform} search template`}><Textarea rows={6} className="dfir-code" value={editor.queries[index]?.query||''} onChange={e=>setEditor({...editor,queries:platforms.map((p,i)=>({platform:p,title:platform+' search',query:i===index?e.target.value:editor.queries[i]?.query||''}))})} placeholder="Use placeholders such as {{IP}}, {{EMAIL}}, {{HOSTNAME}}, {{START}}, {{END}}"/></Field>)}</>}
   </div>}
   <DialogFooter><Button variant="outline" onClick={()=>setEditor(null)}>Cancel</Button><Button onClick={saveItem}>Save resource</Button></DialogFooter>
  </DialogContent></Dialog>
 </section>;
}

function ResourceCard({item,editable,canModify,values,setValues,missing,onCopy,onEdit,onRemove}:{item:ToolkitItem;editable:boolean;canModify:boolean;values:Record<string,string>;setValues:(value:Record<string,string>)=>void;missing:(tokens:string[])=>string[];onCopy:(text:string)=>void;onEdit:(item:ToolkitItem)=>void;onRemove:(item:ToolkitItem)=>void}){
 const [expanded,setExpanded]=useState(item.kind!=='guide');
 const Icon=item.kind==='guide'?BookOpen:item.kind==='playbook'?ShieldAlert:Terminal;
 return <article className="dfir-card"><div className="dfir-card-heading"><div className="dfir-card-icon"><Icon size={19}/></div><div className="dfir-card-title"><div><Badge variant="outline">{item.category||'Uncategorized'}</Badge><span className="dfir-custom-label">{editable?'Custom':'Built-in'}</span></div><h2>{item.title}</h2><p>{item.summary}</p></div><div className="dfir-card-actions">{editable&&<Button size="icon" variant="ghost" aria-label={`Edit ${item.title}`} disabled={!canModify} onClick={()=>onEdit(item)}><Pencil size={16}/></Button>}<Button size="icon" variant="ghost" aria-label={`Delete ${item.title}`} disabled={!canModify} onClick={()=>onRemove(item)}><Trash2 size={16}/></Button></div></div>
  {item.content&&<div className="dfir-content-wrap"><button className="dfir-expand" onClick={()=>setExpanded(!expanded)} aria-expanded={expanded}>{expanded?'Hide details':'Show details'}</button>{expanded&&<pre className="dfir-content">{item.content}</pre>}</div>}
  {item.kind==='playbook'&&<div className="dfir-queries">{item.queries.map(q=><QueryCard key={q.platform} query={q} values={values} setValues={setValues} missing={missing} onCopy={onCopy}/>)}</div>}
  {item.kind==='command'&&<CommandCard item={item} values={values} setValues={setValues} missing={missing} onCopy={onCopy}/>}
 </article>;
}

function QueryCard({query,values,setValues,missing,onCopy}:{query:Query;values:Record<string,string>;setValues:(value:Record<string,string>)=>void;missing:(tokens:string[])=>string[];onCopy:(text:string)=>void}){
 const tokens=placeholders(query.query),unset=missing(tokens),rendered=interpolateText(query.query,values,query.platform);
 return <div className="dfir-query"><div className="dfir-query-head"><div><Badge>{query.platform}</Badge><b>{query.title}</b></div><Button size="sm" variant="outline" disabled={!!unset.length} onClick={()=>onCopy(rendered)}><ClipboardCopy size={15}/>Copy search</Button></div>
  {tokens.length>0&&<div className="dfir-parameters">{tokens.map(token=><Field key={token} label={token.replaceAll('_',' ')}><Input value={values[token]||''} onChange={event=>setValues({...values,[token]:event.target.value})} placeholder={`Enter ${token.toLowerCase()}`}/></Field>)}</div>}
  {unset.length>0&&<div className="dfir-missing">Fill in {unset.join(', ')} to enable copying.</div>}
  <pre className="dfir-code-preview">{rendered}</pre>
 </div>;
}

function CommandCard({item,values,setValues,missing,onCopy}:{item:ToolkitItem;values:Record<string,string>;setValues:(value:Record<string,string>)=>void;missing:(tokens:string[])=>string[];onCopy:(text:string)=>void}){
 const tokens=placeholders(item.command),unset=missing(tokens),rendered=renderCommand(item.command,values);
 return <div className="dfir-command"><div className="dfir-query-head"><b>Command template</b><Button size="sm" variant="outline" disabled={!!unset.length} onClick={()=>onCopy(rendered)}><Copy size={15}/>Copy command</Button></div>
  {tokens.length>0&&<div className="dfir-parameters">{tokens.map(token=><Field key={token} label={token.replaceAll('_',' ')}><Input value={values[token]||''} onChange={event=>setValues({...values,[token]:event.target.value})} placeholder={`Enter ${token.toLowerCase()}`}/></Field>)}</div>}
  {unset.length>0&&<div className="dfir-missing">Fill in {unset.join(', ')} to enable copying.</div>}
  <pre className="dfir-code-preview">{rendered}</pre>
 </div>;
}
